  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">IMGLAD - Advocate Pride in Mindanao</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The previous project generally placed a premium on consolidation by strengthening the knowledge and organizational capacity of IMGLAD chapters, identifying community issues and building a working agenda about these issues, and establishing connections toLGBTQIA+ organizations and partners to ensure that our initiatives will not be in silos from collective LGBTQIA+ actions. The change witnessed initially resided in the idea of having an augmented knowledge and space to voice out issues, exhaust one&#x2019;s talent and capacities,and respond to the prideful call for leadership. But further advocacy skills are needed to push the advocacy and ensure that we arenot only members but advocates of the LGBTQIA+ community. Hence, armed with the necessary advocacy skills, the &#x201c;IMGLAD to Advocate Pride in Mindanao&#x201d; is an initiative that turns IMGLAD Chapters, not as mere vessels but a community of practice where advocates and allies can contribute to widening the political space
for the LGBTQIA+ collective action. Situated in considerably grassroots, remote, and invisible spaces, the skills gained from this
initiative are hoped to spark change as exemplified by advocates and allies proactively voicing out their issues within and beyond their immediate organization, feeding issues to the partners that gained from the previous project, and generally adding texture to our collective narrative. This project also hopes to witness change at the individual level, either as an LGBTQIA+ person or ally, to see him/her/they not only as a member or friend of an immediate organization but as part of a larger rainbow coalition and community of advocates working for the SOGIESC equality. It also hopes to witness change within the organization seen not only as a vessel of dormant issues and assemblage of functions and positions but as an active collective agent that feeds and interjects its agenda to the larger coalition of gender and human rights defenders where it is a part. And, albeit no direct change to the immediate, the output(improved knowledge, enhanced capacity, stronger allies, etc.) will form a crucial part of the brewing calls for LGBTQIA+ enabling policies and ordinances in the areas we work with.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="90">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">BALAOD Mindanaw Inc (Balay Alternative Legal Advocates for Development in Mindanaw, Inc.) is the new Host Organization of our grantee-partner, IM GLAD, for this new EM Accelerated Grant.

BALAOD Mindanaw Inc. works with marginalized sectors and communities in Mindanaw through alternative lawyering and paralegalism, stressing active non-violence as an integral part thereof in the advancement of justice, gender equality, and resource tenure improvement in the context of active people&#x2019;s participation in Governance.

It is a non-stock, non-profit legal resource institution that provides legal services and capacity-building programs to its partner communities on resource tenure issues, human rights, women and children protection, environment protection and other justice issues primarily in Mindanao. It was formally established and registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) on August 11, 2000 through the initiative of a small group of advocates, lawyers, paralegals and community organizers.
As an institution, &#x201c;BALAOD,&#x201d; which isalso the Visayan word for law, believes in the legal empowerment of the marginalized groups and communities as a means to advance their access to justice, gender equality, and resource tenure improvement in the context of active peoples&#x2019; participation in governance. To this end, BALAOD engages with various marginalized groups through paralegal formation and capacity-building, alternative lawyering, policy formulation and advocacy guided by active non-violence as integral principle thereto. Hence, &#x201c;bringing the law in the hands of the marginalized sectors and communities.&#x201d;</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Balay Alternative Legal Advocates</narrative>
